1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,257
Radio spectrum is real estate — and bandwidth
is how much of it a signal occupies.

2
00:00:07,177 --> 00:00:12,005
A narrowband voice channel takes about
twelve and a half kilohertz of road.

3
00:00:11,925 --> 00:00:16,881
An FM broadcast station, two hundred
kilohertz — sixteen times wider.

4
00:00:16,801 --> 00:00:19,690
A Wi-Fi channel, tens of megahertz.

5
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:27,700
Bandwidth decides how fast information can flow, how many signals
fit side by side, and how much spectrum a receiver has to swallow.

6
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:30,544
It may be the most consequential number in
radio.

7
00:00:30,694 --> 00:00:36,788
Formally: bandwidth is the width, in hertz,
of the frequency range a signal occupies.

8
00:00:36,938 --> 00:00:39,165
Why does a signal have width at all?

9
00:00:39,085 --> 00:00:43,518
An unmodulated carrier is a single
spectral line — zero width.

10
00:00:43,438 --> 00:00:50,924
The moment you modulate it, energy spreads into sidebands on either
side, and the faster the message changes, the wider they spread.

11
00:00:50,844 --> 00:00:52,549
Information takes room.

12
00:00:52,469 --> 00:00:55,498
That's not an engineering flaw; it's
physics.

13
00:00:55,418 --> 00:01:06,120
Regulators then draw lane markings: each service gets a channel of a defined width, with
guard bands of empty spectrum between lanes to keep neighbors from sideswiping each other.

14
00:01:06,270 --> 00:01:09,078
And here's the law that makes bandwidth
precious.

15
00:01:08,998 --> 00:01:18,505
Shannon's theorem sets the ceiling on any channel's data rate: capacity
equals bandwidth times the log of one plus the signal-to-noise ratio.

16
00:01:18,425 --> 00:01:20,211
Look at the shape of that formula.

17
00:01:20,131 --> 00:01:25,051
Capacity grows linearly with bandwidth —
double the width, double the ceiling.

18
00:01:24,971 --> 00:01:30,194
But only logarithmically with signal power —
doubling your SNR buys you a mere fraction more.

19
00:01:30,114 --> 00:01:36,916
If you want more data, more bandwidth is the straight road,
which is exactly why spectrum auctions raise billions.

20
00:01:37,066 --> 00:01:39,235
Bandwidth cuts the other way too.

21
00:01:39,155 --> 00:01:46,560
Noise power grows with every hertz you listen to — open your receiver's
filter wider than the signal and all you admit is more hiss.

22
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:55,058
The craft on the receive side is matching the filter to the signal: wide
enough to pass it undistorted, narrow enough to shut out everything else.

23
00:01:54,978 --> 00:01:59,144
Keep that idea — it becomes the star of
the signal-to-noise chapter.

24
00:01:59,294 --> 00:02:02,683
GopherTrunk lives on both sides of this
trade at once.

25
00:02:02,603 --> 00:02:11,865
Its SDR captures a slice several megahertz wide — wide enough to watch a
trunked system's control channel and all its voice channels simultaneously.

26
00:02:11,785 --> 00:02:20,201
Then digital down-converters carve out each narrow twelve-and-a-half-kilohertz
channel and filter it down to just the bandwidth the decoder needs.

27
00:02:20,121 --> 00:02:24,090
Wide capture to see everything; narrow
channels to decode cleanly.

28
00:02:24,010 --> 00:02:26,074
That split is trunk-tracking.

29
00:02:27,024 --> 00:02:36,937
So: bandwidth is a signal's footprint on the spectrum, the linear lever in Shannon's
speed limit, and — on receive — a filter you keep just as narrow as you can.

30
00:02:36,857 --> 00:02:38,922
Full write-up linked below.

